      "text": "Collegeville’s name predates Ursinus College, named after the earlier Pennsylvania Female College that closed in 1880.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collegeville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 18th‑century Perkiomen Bridge, an 18‑story stone arch, still spans the creek and is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collegeville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ursinus College opened its doors in 1869, becoming the first four‑year liberal arts college in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collegeville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Collegeville’s streets still echo colonial‑era infrastructure, offering quiet, uncrowded exploration for history lovers.",
